<div class="row">
	<div class="col-sm-12 tadl-about-content">
		<h1>About the Local History Collection</h1>

		<h2>Preserving Our Past, Connecting Our Community</h2>
		<p>The Traverse Area District Library (TADL) Local History Collection (LHC) is dedicated to preserving and sharing the history of Grand Traverse County and the surrounding region. Our collections document the people, places, organizations, and events that have shaped our region through archival materials, including but not limited to photographs, documents, newspapers, maps, oral histories, and born-digital objects. These materials are held in the public trust, and we are committed to expanding opportunities for everyone to explore, learn from, and use them.</p>

		<h2>Frequently Asked Questions: Use of the Collection, Citations, Additional Resources, and More</h2>
		<p>Interested in using the collections beyond viewing the online catalog? Visit our <a href="<?= htmlspecialchars(caNavUrl($this->request, '', 'Front', 'Index').'#tadl-faq-heading', ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8'); ?>">Frequently Asked Questions</a> page for information about accessing archival materials, additional research resources available through TADL, and answers to common questions about using our collections.</p>

		<h2>Acknowledging Our Region's History</h2>
		<p>The history of the Grand Traverse region extends far beyond the period documented by much of our collection. We recognize the Anishinaabek, including the Odawa and Ojibwe peoples, whose ancestral homelands include this region and whose communities, cultures, and traditions remain an important part of its history and present. While our archival holdings include some materials relating to Indigenous history, they do not comprehensively document the experiences and perspectives of the region's Indigenous peoples. We recognize that these histories are best understood through many sources, including the knowledge, records, and perspectives of Indigenous communities themselves.</p>

		<h2>What We Collect</h2>
		<p>The LHC preserves historical records documenting the people, places, organizations, and events of Grand Traverse County and, when appropriate, the greater Grand Traverse region. We work collaboratively with other regional institutions dedicated to preserving local history, maintaining complementary collecting priorities that support our shared mission.</p>
		<p>Our holdings include photographs, maps, correspondence, diaries, organizational records, oral histories, architectural drawings, scrapbooks, newspapers, born-digital objects, and other materials that help tell the story of our communities.</p>
		<p>Our collections have developed over time through donations, acquisitions, and the preservation efforts of library staff and community members. As a result, they reflect both the historical record and the circumstances under which materials were collected and preserved. While we strive to preserve a broad range of local experiences and perspectives, our holdings do not represent a complete history of the region. Some communities, individuals, and historical experiences are documented more extensively than others. For additional information about our collecting priorities, please see TADL's <a href="https://www.tadl.org/policies/107-collection-materials-selection-policy">Collection Materials Selection Policy</a>.</p>

		<h2>A Note About Historical Content</h2>
		<p>The LHC preserves materials that reflect the people, events, language, and attitudes of the periods in which they were created. Some records may contain images, descriptions, or language that are offensive, discriminatory, or harmful, including racist, sexist, ableist, or homophobic content. We preserve these materials for their historical and research value, recognizing that understanding the past requires access to its records, including those that reflect difficult or painful aspects of our shared history. The inclusion of these materials in the Local History Collection does not imply endorsement of the views, language, or attitudes they may contain by TADL. Our commitment is to preserve the historical record while providing meaningful access to the many experiences and perspectives that have shaped our communities.</p>
